# The Riser - a plumber, a leak that is older than it looks, and the form that decides what it says.
# Written for the library. One person, one procedure, one decision: what to put in writing about a fault that
# somebody has been living with for a long time.

title: The Riser
start: start

== start ==
bg hall_corner
music room_tone
Two in the morning, fourth floor of a block of flats, and the caretaker is
holding a torch because the stair lights are on a timer nobody has reset since
the clocks went back.
The water is off at the rising main. You did that first, before you took your
coat off, because that is the order you do it in: stop it, then go and look.
Under the door of 4C the carpet is dark in a strip about a hand wide.
-> the_landing

== the_landing ==
The block is six floors of the same plan. You have been here eleven times in
four years: a burst on three, a blocked stack on five, and twice for the same
flat, which is a thing you notice and do not write down.
The caretaker's name is on a card by the lift: D. Whyte. The card is older than
the lift.
whyte: It's coming through the ceiling of 3C as well. That's why they rang.
whyte: 4C is the one that's awake. He's been up. He's been up a while, actually.
* Knock on 4C. -> four_c
* Find the riser for this stack first. -> the_riser

== the_riser ==
bg closet
The riser cupboard is at the end of the landing, painted the same as the wall so
that nobody opens it by accident. Inside: four risers, a stopcock, a meter that
has not been read since it was installed, and a smell of wet plaster that is not
from tonight.
You put your hand on the pipe for 4C and it is wet from the floor up to about
your shoulder, and the paint has gone the way paint goes when it has been wet
and dried and wet again. There is a tide line. There are four tide lines.
You take a photograph of that, because you are thorough, and then you stand
there for a moment in a small room with a torch, doing arithmetic about how long
a pipe has to be seeping for the plaster to look like that.
* Knock on 4C now. -> four_c
* Write the tide lines down while you are looking at them. -> write_it_down

== write_it_down ==
You write it in your notebook: *4C riser, wet to shoulder, four tide lines,
plaster blown.* Not for anybody - you write the same way you photograph, so that
in a week you are not guessing.
Then you go and knock on 4C, because he is awake and awake is a gift at this
hour.
-> four_c

== four_c ==
bg east_hall
The man at 4C is about seventy and dressed, at two in the morning, in a
cardigan, which tells you he was up before the water came through and the water
is not why he is awake.
tenant: It's the wall in the bathroom. It's been damp since the spring. I rang
about it in April.
you: Who did you ring?
tenant: The managing agent. Twice. They sent somebody, he looked at the ceiling
in 3C and said it was condensation.
He stands back to let you in, and then says the thing that is actually on his
mind, and says it fast, the way people do.
tenant: If you write that it's been there since April, they'll say it's the
wear and tear and it's mine to pay for. If you write tonight, it's theirs.
* Ask what he wants you to write. -> ask_him
* Look at the wall first, and decide after. -> the_wall
* Tell him what you are going to write and why. -> the_truth

== ask_him ==
you: What do you want me to put on the sheet.
tenant: I want you to put the truth.
you: The truth is a number, and the number is April.
tenant: Then put April.
He says it without hesitating, which you were not expecting, and you have been
doing this job for nineteen years and you know exactly what happens to people
who put April in a box on a form. The managing agent's process has a step in it
for this, and the step costs four hundred pounds and it is called chargeable.
you: That'll be a survey. At your cost, initially. Then they'll decide.
tenant: Then they'll decide.
~ told_the_truth +1
-> the_report

== the_wall ==
bg restrooms
The bathroom is a small room that has been repainted, and repainted again, and
the paper is the kind that is meant to hide things. You peel a corner back with
your thumb, which you are not supposed to do, and the plaster underneath is
black and soft and cold.
The leak is above the bath, at the joint where the riser goes through the floor,
and it is a two-hour repair in daylight and a nine-hour one now, and it has been
dripping long enough that the floorboards under the bath have gone.
You put the paper back down. From the doorway it looks the way it looked when
you came in.
* Write tonight, and say nothing about the paper. -> ending_quiet
* Write April, and let the survey happen. -> ending_april
* Write tonight, and tell the man what you actually found. -> the_report
~ wrote_the_truth +1

== the_truth ==
you: I'm going to write what I found, which is tonight's leak at the joint and
plaster that has been wet for a long time. I'm going to date the tide lines.
you: I'm not going to say whose fault it is, because that's not my box on the
form either. Somebody will decide.
tenant: And if they decide it's mine.
you: Then they'll have to decide that out loud, to you, in writing.
He looks at you for a moment, and then goes to put the kettle on, which is what
people do when they have decided to trust somebody.
tenant: It's a shared riser. It always was.
~ wrote_the_truth +1
-> the_report

== the_report ==
You fill the sheet in at the van with the interior light on, because it is
half past three and you are not going to do this twice.
Three boxes matter: DATE OF FAULT, which is a date and not a time; ACCESS, which
is who has to be in; and DESCRIPTION, which is four lines and has been the same
four lines on every one of these for nineteen years, because the process wants a
description of the pipe and not of the last eleven months.
Tonight the description runs over into the margin.
-> the_choice

== the_choice ==
The pen is a cheap one and the paper is thin, and above the margin it says,
in eight-point type, that a deliberately misleading report is a disciplinary
matter.
if wrote_the_truth >= 1 -> ending_named
if told_the_truth >= 1 -> ending_april
-> ending_quiet

== ending_named ==
You write the tide lines down as lines, with dates against them where you can
read the dates, and you write that the joint failed tonight. You do not write
whose fault it is, because there is no box for that and there never was.
It goes in on Thursday. The managing agent asks for a survey, and the survey
says what your sheet said, because you were careful and there is nothing in it
to argue with.
The man at 4C rings the office in November to say the wall has been done. He says
thank you the way people say it when they have been right about something for
eight months.
-> END

== ending_april ==
You put April in the box, because he asked you to and because it is true, and the
rest of the sheet is tonight, and the two do not fit on one form, so the form
takes the date you gave it and the process does what the process does.
It comes back as chargeable. There is a survey, and the survey is his, and the
letter that arrives in October has a reference number on it and a payment slip.
He does not ring the office. You look the job up in December to see how it ended,
which is a thing you do, and it ended the way the form said it would.
-> END

== ending_quiet ==
You put tonight in the box and you put a new joint in the description, and the
form is exactly the form it was always going to be, and nobody argues with it.
The repair is authorised in a week. The wall is done. The man at 4C says thank
you at the door in the way people do when they have decided not to say the rest.
You put the photograph of the tide lines in the folder with the others, which is
not evidence of anything and not yours to send, and nineteen years is a long time
and you have got very good at the four lines.
-> END
